caption: Soul Identity

Souls have the spirit template. They are not insubstantial in afterlife dimensions, but are otherwise exactly like normal spirits (and, at the GMs discretion, spirits are just souls as well). They exist eternally unless consumed by a god.

**Temporal selves:** Recognizable temporal versions of one person share one higher-soul identity rather than creating additional souls. While more than one recognizable temporal self simultaneously remains alive, no individual body has a separately available higher soul for Sacrifice, Demon Lord kill credit, afterlife capture, Torment custody, Consumption, or any other soul-derived reward. Killing one temporal self yields no soul or Godmath credit while another temporal self survives; the shared soul remains with the surviving temporal identity. Only when the final simultaneously extant temporal self dies does the one shared soul become available, producing at most one soul and one associated sacrifice or other soul-derived reward. When time travel makes an earlier death or other soul-state event causally valid, mutually incompatible later sacrifices, afterlife transfers, custody states, torment, consumption, and soul-derived rewards are superseded. Single-soul accounting overrides Traveler Continuity for those soul states and rewards. Temporal Inertia may preserve memory of superseded events, but never creates a second soul or a duplicate Godmath payout. Use the current **GATES Time Travel Rules** for the complete temporal-self and soul-worldline procedure.
